 Kubernetes CNI Error? Here’s What I Faced & Fixed!

While setting up my Kubernetes cluster using kubeadm, I ran into a common but confusing issue related to CNI (Container Network Interface) in Kubernetes.

❌ Error I Faced:

NetworkPluginNotReady: network plugin is not ready: cni config uninitialized.

Pods were stuck in Pending state and CoreDNS wasn’t starting 😓

🔍 Root Cause:

The CNI plugin was not installed after initializing the cluster using kubeadm init.

Kubernetes does NOT install a network plugin by default — we must install one manually.

✅ Solution:

I installed a CNI plugin (Flannel in my case):

kubectl apply -f https://lnkd.in/d63tsU6G

After that:

✔️ CoreDNS started running
✔️ Nodes became Ready
✔️ Pods started communicating properly

💡 Lesson Learned:

Always install a CNI plugin immediately after cluster initialization.

Popular CNI options:

🔹 Calico
🔹 Flannel
🔹 Weave Net

Understanding networking in Kubernetes is 🔑 for every DevOps Engineer.
